{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GXAPzZ6arVavk5ZBYhmsMV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t \"number\": 89,\n3\t \"title\": \"WebAuthn request that needs a security-key PIN crashes the browser process on Linux\",\n4\t \"body\": \"### Preflight Checklist\\n\\n- [x] I have read the [Contributing Guidelines](https://github.com/electron/electron/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md) for this project.\\n- [x] I agree to follow the [Code of Conduct](https://github.com/electron/electron/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md) that this project adheres to.\\n- [x] I have searched the [issue tracker](https://www.github.com/electron/electron/issues) for a bug report that matches the one I want to file, without success.\\n\\n### Electron Version\\n\\n43.4.0\\n\\n### What operating system(s) are you using?\\n\\nOther Linux\\n\\n### Operating System Version\\n\\nCachyOS (Arch-based), Linux 7.1.8-1-cachyos, KDE Plasma 6 (Wayland)\\n\\n### What arch are you using?\\n\\nx64\\n\\n### Last Known Working Electron version\\n\\nNone found. 43.4.1, 42.9.0, and 39.8.10 also crashed with SIGTRAP, but those runs followed an earlier crash that had left the YubiKey 5 unresponsive, so they are consistent with the finding rather than clean data. The clean single-key runs below are on 43.4.0.\\n\\n### Does the issue also appear in Chromium / Google Chrome?\\n\\nI don't know how to test\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen a WebAuthn request reaches a USB security key that requires a PIN and Electron has no PIN prompt to show, the request should reject (for example with `NotAllowedError`) and the app should keep running.\\n\\n### Actual Behavior\\n\\nThe browser process dies with SIGTRAP from a CHECK about 0.7 s after launch, during `navigator.credentials.create()` and before the key is touched. With `--enable-logging=stderr --vmodule=device_event_log_impl=1`, the FIDO log ends right after Chromium reads the key's PIN retry count:\\n\\n```\\nFIDO: DEBUG: fido_device.cc:70 The device supports the CTAP2 protocol.\\nFIDO: DEBUG: ctap2_device_operation.h:91 <- 0x6 (kAuthenticatorClientPin) {1: 2, 2: 7}\\nFIDO: DEBUG: ctap2_device_operation.h:188 -> {5: 0}\\nFIDO: DEBUG: ctap2_device_operation.h:91 <- 0x6 (kAuthenticatorClientPin) {1: 2, 2: 1}\\nFIDO: DEBUG: ctap2_device_operation.h:188 -> {3: 8}\\n```\\n\\n`{5: 0}` answers the UV-retries query and `{3: 8}` reports 8 PIN retries.\\n\\nResults with the gist on 43.4.0:\\n\\n| Keys plugged in | Result |\\n|---|---|\\n| YubiKey 5 only | Crash during `create()`, no touch |\\n| YubiKey 4 only | `create()` OK, `get()` OK |\\n| Both; YubiKey 4 touched | `create()` OK, `get()` OK; the log shows a clean cancel of the YubiKey 5's operation |\\n\\nThe YubiKey 5 (USB `1050:0406`, firmware 5.8.0) reports `alwaysUv: true`, `uv: true`, `clientPin: true`, `bioEnroll: true`, `makeCredUvNotRqd: false`, and `pinUvAuthToken: true`, so every operation needs user verification. The YubiKey 4 (USB `1050:0407`) is U2F only.\\n\\n### Testcase Gist URL\\n\\nhttps://gist.github.com/nisavid/a7e5438b6ceef5a0761898dc99ec6b64\\n\\n### Additional Information\\n\\nThe gist is a minimal main-process app that serves `index.html` on `http://localhost` and runs `navigator.credentials.create()` for a non-resident credential with `userVerification: \\\"discouraged\\\"`, then `navigator.credentials.get()` with that credential. `get()` takes `userVerification` from the `uv` query parameter (default `discouraged`); `REPRO_QUERY='?uv=preferred'` sets it, which the crashing runs used, though the single-key run crashed inside `create()` before `get()` ran. Run it with a PIN-protected FIDO2 key plugged in:\\n\\n```\\nelectron . --enable-logging=stderr --vmodule=device_event_log_impl=1\\n```\\n\\nThis is narrower than #24573, which tracks the missing Linux FIDO2 PIN UI: here a request that needs the PIN takes the browser process down instead of rejecting. It surfaced in the Proton Pass desktop app (`/usr/lib/electron43/electron /usr/share/proton-pass/app.asar`), whose in-app sign-in crashed three times on 2026-09-24 with a main-thread stack identical to the gist's. Chrome was not tested.\\n\\n<details><summary>In-depth investigation</summary>\\n<p>\\n\\nThe distro `electron43` binary is stripped. `systemd-coredump` labels frame 0 `cppgc::internal::FatalImpl`, but that 7-byte exported symbol (`push rbp; mov rbp,rsp; int3; ud2`) is an identical-code-folded `IMMEDIATE_CRASH` stub, so the name is a misattribution. Identifying the other frames by the source-path strings their functions reference gives: a `device.mojom.HidConnection` read, then `device/fido/hid/fido_hid_device.cc` (message received), then the `device/fido/ctap2_device_operation.h` response callback, then the crash stub. The gist's core has the same stack hash as the Proton Pass cores.\\n\\nMy reading, as inference: the key's `alwaysUv` makes the request need PIN/UV, the request handler moves on to PIN collection after reading the retry counts, and with no PIN UI on Linux the browser process hits a CHECK instead of failing the request.\\n\\n</p>\\n</details>\",\n5\t \"labels\": [\n6\t  \"43-x-y\",\n7\t  \"bug :beetle:\",\n8\t  \"has-repro-gist\",\n9\t  \"platform/linux\"\n10\t ],\n11\t \"state\": \"open\",\n12\t \"author_association\": \"NONE\",\n13\t \"as_of\": \"2026-09-24T21:17:47Z\",\n14\t \"replay\": true\n15\t}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"50f383af-a61f-4a7f-816f-b411a3655d1c","uuid":"57bd0608-0165-4122-94f2-d5d99a46298c","timestamp":"2026-10-23T10:04:08.314Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/runner/work/cs467-electron-triage/cs467-electron-triage/.triage/issue.json","content":"{\n \"number\": 89,\n \"title\": \"WebAuthn request that needs a security-key PIN crashes the browser process on Linux\",\n \"body\": \"### Preflight Checklist\\n\\n- [x] I have read the [Contributing Guidelines](https://github.com/electron/electron/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md) for this project.\\n- [x] I agree to follow the [Code of Conduct](https://github.com/electron/electron/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md) that this project adheres to.\\n- [x] I have searched the [issue tracker](https://www.github.com/electron/electron/issues) for a bug report that matches the one I want to file, without success.\\n\\n### Electron Version\\n\\n43.4.0\\n\\n### What operating system(s) are you using?\\n\\nOther Linux\\n\\n### Operating System Version\\n\\nCachyOS (Arch-based), Linux 7.1.8-1-cachyos, KDE Plasma 6 (Wayland)\\n\\n### What arch are you using?\\n\\nx64\\n\\n### Last Known Working Electron version\\n\\nNone found. 43.4.1, 42.9.0, and 39.8.10 also crashed with SIGTRAP, but those runs followed an earlier crash that had left the YubiKey 5 unresponsive, so they are consistent with the finding rather than clean data. The clean single-key runs below are on 43.4.0.\\n\\n### Does the issue also appear in Chromium / Google Chrome?\\n\\nI don't know how to test\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen a WebAuthn request reaches a USB security key that requires a PIN and Electron has no PIN prompt to show, the request should reject (for example with `NotAllowedError`) and the app should keep running.\\n\\n### Actual Behavior\\n\\nThe browser process dies with SIGTRAP from a CHECK about 0.7 s after launch, during `navigator.credentials.create()` and before the key is touched. With `--enable-logging=stderr --vmodule=device_event_log_impl=1`, the FIDO log ends right after Chromium reads the key's PIN retry count:\\n\\n```\\nFIDO: DEBUG: fido_device.cc:70 The device supports the CTAP2 protocol.\\nFIDO: DEBUG: ctap2_device_operation.h:91 <- 0x6 (kAuthenticatorClientPin) {1: 2, 2: 7}\\nFIDO: DEBUG: ctap2_device_operation.h:188 -> {5: 0}\\nFIDO: DEBUG: ctap2_device_operation.h:91 <- 0x6 (kAuthenticatorClientPin) {1: 2, 2: 1}\\nFIDO: DEBUG: ctap2_device_operation.h:188 -> {3: 8}\\n```\\n\\n`{5: 0}` answers the UV-retries query and `{3: 8}` reports 8 PIN retries.\\n\\nResults with the gist on 43.4.0:\\n\\n| Keys plugged in | Result |\\n|---|---|\\n| YubiKey 5 only | Crash during `create()`, no touch |\\n| YubiKey 4 only | `create()` OK, `get()` OK |\\n| Both; YubiKey 4 touched | `create()` OK, `get()` OK; the log shows a clean cancel of the YubiKey 5's operation |\\n\\nThe YubiKey 5 (USB `1050:0406`, firmware 5.8.0) reports `alwaysUv: true`, `uv: true`, `clientPin: true`, `bioEnroll: true`, `makeCredUvNotRqd: false`, and `pinUvAuthToken: true`, so every operation needs user verification. The YubiKey 4 (USB `1050:0407`) is U2F only.\\n\\n### Testcase Gist URL\\n\\nhttps://gist.github.com/nisavid/a7e5438b6ceef5a0761898dc99ec6b64\\n\\n### Additional Information\\n\\nThe gist is a minimal main-process app that serves `index.html` on `http://localhost` and runs `navigator.credentials.create()` for a non-resident credential with `userVerification: \\\"discouraged\\\"`, then `navigator.credentials.get()` with that credential. `get()` takes `userVerification` from the `uv` query parameter (default `discouraged`); `REPRO_QUERY='?uv=preferred'` sets it, which the crashing runs used, though the single-key run crashed inside `create()` before `get()` ran. Run it with a PIN-protected FIDO2 key plugged in:\\n\\n```\\nelectron . --enable-logging=stderr --vmodule=device_event_log_impl=1\\n```\\n\\nThis is narrower than #24573, which tracks the missing Linux FIDO2 PIN UI: here a request that needs the PIN takes the browser process down instead of rejecting. It surfaced in the Proton Pass desktop app (`/usr/lib/electron43/electron /usr/share/proton-pass/app.asar`), whose in-app sign-in crashed three times on 2026-09-24 with a main-thread stack identical to the gist's. Chrome was not tested.\\n\\n<details><summary>In-depth investigation</summary>\\n<p>\\n\\nThe distro `electron43` binary is stripped. `systemd-coredump` labels frame 0 `cppgc::internal::FatalImpl`, but that 7-byte exported symbol (`push rbp; mov rbp,rsp; int3; ud2`) is an identical-code-folded `IMMEDIATE_CRASH` stub, so the name is a misattribution. Identifying the other frames by the source-path strings their functions reference gives: a `device.mojom.HidConnection` read, then `device/fido/hid/fido_hid_device.cc` (message received), then the `device/fido/ctap2_device_operation.h` response callback, then the crash stub. The gist's core has the same stack hash as the Proton Pass cores.\\n\\nMy reading, as inference: the key's `alwaysUv` makes the request need PIN/UV, the request handler moves on to PIN collection after reading the retry counts, and with no PIN UI on Linux the browser process hits a CHECK instead of failing the request.\\n\\n</p>\\n</details>\",\n \"labels\": [\n  \"43-x-y\",\n  \"bug :beetle:\",\n  \"has-repro-gist\",\n  \"platform/linux\"\n ],\n \"state\": \"open\",\n \"author_association\": \"NONE\",\n \"as_of\": \"2026-09-24T21:17:47Z\",\n \"replay\": true\n}","numLines":15,"startLine":1,"totalLines":15}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01GXAPzZ6arVavk5ZBYhmsMV","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
